"He applied the method to belief itself. There are questions, he held, where the evidence is incomplete, the choice cannot be put off, and much depends on it. Whether another person can be trusted, whether a risky effort will succeed, and whether life is worth living are questions of this kind. In such cases waiting for proof is itself a choice, and belief can be among the causes of the fact. A climber who must leap a gap is more likely to clear it if he believes he can. So with the worth of life: Believe that life is worth living, and your belief will help create the fact. He took care to say that this license holds only where evidence cannot settle the matter. It gives no permission to believe against the evidence.": "7b72aa91bd26",
"He drew from this a lesson that he asked teachers in particular to remember. Each of us feels intensely the importance of his own concerns, and each is blind to the inner significance of lives unlike his own. The blindness he meant is the blindness with which we all are afflicted in regard to the feelings of creatures and people different from ourselves. The conclusion he drew is modest and hard to follow. We should not be quick to pronounce on the worth of other peoples ways of living, since we stand outside them. This sits uneasily beside any confident list of what makes a life meaningful, including the one in the lesson on that subject. James, who believed that meaning requires an ideal, knew that a spectator is poorly placed to see whether someone else has one.": "ca6f0dc2c481",
"He had married Alice Gibbens in 1878, and the marriage steadied him. They had five children, one of whom died in infancy. His students at Harvard included Theodore Roosevelt, Gertrude Stein, and W. E. B. Du Bois, and he was known for treating them as equals. Having finished the great book, he announced that he was tired of psychology and turned to the questions that had driven him since his breakdown.": "5c1c59296d2b",
"He took his medical degree in 1869 and never practiced. His back, his eyes, and his digestion troubled him, and behind these lay a depression that brought him close to suicide. The science he had studied seemed to show that human beings are machines and that effort is an illusion. On April 30, 1870, he wrote in his diary that he had been reading the French philosopher Renouvier and saw no reason why free will need be an illusion. My first act of free will shall be to believe in free will. He recovered slowly, and he always spoke of that decision as the turning point.": "2d22bb512f3b",
"His practical teaching is closer still. Habit, he told teachers, is formed by repeated acts, and character is the sum of habits, so that every small choice leaves its trace. That is the working of karma described in the vocabulary of physiology. He held that what we attend to becomes our world, and that the power to bring a wandering attention back, again and again, is the root of judgment and will. He added that he did not know how such a power could be trained. The Buddhist tradition would say that this is what the practice of mindfulness is for. The two part company on the end in view. James wanted a strenuous life given to chosen ideals. The Buddha taught the stilling of the craving that makes us strain. Each would have seen the others point (Dharma and Western Philosophy D3).": "9b5ca3c4b9f9",
"In 1873 Harvard gave him a post teaching physiology. He moved the subject toward the mind, set up one of the first psychological laboratories in the world, and in 1878 signed a contract to write a textbook in two years. It took twelve. The Principles of Psychology, published in 1890, runs to fourteen hundred pages and is written with a novelists eye. It described consciousness as a stream and not a chain of separate ideas, argued that we feel afraid because we tremble and not the other way about, and gave a chapter to habit, the enormous fly-wheel of society, on which it founded a theory of education. The great thing, then, in all education, is to make our nervous system our ally instead of our enemy.": "dc9d8fef8115",
"In 1907 he gave the name pragmatism, which he credited to his friend Charles Peirce, to a method and a theory of truth, and spent his last years defending it against critics on both sides of the Atlantic. A weak heart, strained years earlier on a climb in the Adirondacks, grew worse. He died at his summer house in New Hampshire in August 1910. His brother Henry, the novelist, was with him.": "9078b286eca3",
"James read what was available on Buddhism in his day and drew on it in The Varieties of Religious Experience, where he treats it with respect as a religion of deliverance that needs no creator god. The closer meeting is in his psychology. His account of consciousness as a stream, in which no state recurs and each passing thought takes up what went before, set aside the idea of a soul that owns the thoughts. The thoughts themselves are the thinkers, he suggested. A Buddhist reader finds here, reached by observation in a Harvard study, something very like the teaching that there is a continuity of moments of mind with no self behind it. A story that he told a visiting Buddhist monk that this was the psychology everyone would be studying in twenty-five years is often repeated, and its source is uncertain.": "d83a76c7e4f2",
"James thought he had been misread, and in part he had. His conditions were strict. The option must be live, forced, and momentous, and the evidence must be unable to decide. In such cases Cliffords rule is not neutral either. It expresses a preference for avoiding error over gaining truth, and that preference is itself a passion. On the theory of truth James was more exposed, and his looser statements, such as that the true is only the expedient in the way of our thinking, invited the reading Russell gave them. His better statement was that true ideas are those we can verify, and that verification happens over the whole course of experience. A comforting falsehood fails that test in the long run. Whether the long run is enough is the point at which pragmatists still divide.": "efa69881a9c4",
"James told his student audience about a journey in the mountains of North Carolina. He passed clearings where settlers had girdled and killed the trees, burned the stumps, and put up rough cabins and zigzag fences among the charred remains. He thought it hideous, a scene of pure loss, and said so to his driver. The man answered that they were never happy there until they had got one of these coves under cultivation. James saw that he had missed the whole meaning of what was in front of him. To the people who made it, the clearing was a record of struggle and a home won for their children.": "ec7e9bc8ea4e",
"James was the eldest child of a wealthy and restless father, a follower of the mystic Swedenborg, who moved his family back and forth across the Atlantic in search of the perfect education. The children learned languages and saw galleries and never stayed at one school for long. At eighteen William decided to be a painter and studied for a year in a studio at Newport. He gave it up, went to Harvard to study chemistry, changed to medicine, and broke off to join an expedition up the Amazon, where he fell ill and concluded that he was not meant to be a naturalist either.": "e80577873928",
"Jamess pragmatism is first of all a method for settling disputes that seem endless. Take any two views and ask what practical difference it would make to anyones experience if one were true and not the other. If no difference can be found, the dispute is idle. If one can, we have learned what the dispute is really about. Ideas, on this approach, are instruments. An idea is tested as a tool is tested, by what it enables us to do and to expect.": "e29d90e895ed",
"The essays of the 1890s defended the right to believe where evidence cannot decide and a decision cannot wait. In 1899 he published his lectures to schoolteachers, with three addresses to students added, among them On a Certain Blindness in Human Beings and What Makes a Life Significant. His Gifford Lectures at Edinburgh became The Varieties of Religious Experience, which studied conversion, saintliness, and mysticism as facts of human psychology, from the testimony of those who had them. He did not ask first whether the beliefs were true.": "20ccfc6a58b7",
"The mathematician W. K. Clifford had laid down a rule in 1877: it is wrong always, everywhere, and for anyone, to believe anything upon insufficient evidence. A shipowner who talks himself into trusting an unsound ship is guilty even if the ship comes home. James wrote The Will to Believe against this rule, and his critics replied that he had written a defense of wishful thinking. Bertrand Russell put it sharply. Jamess theory of truth, he said, makes it is true that other people exist mean it is useful to believe that other people exist, and these are different claims. A belief can be comforting and false. If truth is what works, the question works for whom? has no answer that is not arbitrary.": "592ea4a5ce38",
